    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (76.11) and **Christian Yelich** (73.14) serving as our primary offensive engines. Both outfielders benefit from facing favorable matchups against opponents with slow deliveries, creating high-upside environments for stolen bases\u2014our most critical scarcity category. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** effectively exploits a soft matchup against Fedde (5.37 xERA), and despite an unfavorable #8 spot in the order, his speed-centric profile remains locked in. Similarly, **Christian Yelich** is positioned to feast on Ober, leveraging a 13% dynamic platoon advantage and a slow-delivery profile that keeps our SB ceiling high, even with a minor wind-in penalty. **Carson Benge** also earns a strong recommendation (67.24) by drawing the most favorable matchup of the day against E. Rodr\u00edguez (6.75 xERA), maximizing his base projection through superior SP conditions.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Chase Meidroth** (44.49) on the bench. He hovered near the marginal threshold but ultimately failed to displace the higher-efficiency profiles of **Junior Caminero** (61.60) or **Colt Keith** (55.89). While **Chase Meidroth** offers decent depth, his projected output was suppressed by unfavorable wind conditions and a lack of elite categorical upside compared to our starters. We are closely monitoring the lineup for **Luke Raley**, whose assumed #5 spot provides the necessary leverage for a 58.38 score against Giolito. Conversely, we advise caution regarding the top of our order; **Trea Turner** (49.79), **Yordan Alvarez** (55.90), and **Kyle Schwarber** (48.24) face a daunting challenge against Skenes (2.96 xERA). While their elite talent justifies the start, the SP skill penalty from Skenes\u2014paired with the difficulty of hitting against such a high-velocity arm\u2014dampens our expectations for this trio today.",
